}ncarr@star.enet.dec.com () paraphrased as:
}> [not much demand for Memory Channel on workstations, qualified
}> for servers from biggest first on down]

}This, however, I don't buy. Please notice what I wrote regarding initial
}information on the product, which I looked up as soon as the IEEE Micro (or
}was it Computer?) article on the memory channel reached my mailbox. The
}emphasis was heavily on scientific and parallel computing, mentioned an
}optimized version of PVM almost in the first sentence, talked about the
}underlying, light-weight communications mechanism, etc. No mention of databses
}or such, no talk of large systems - hey, it's currently limited to eight
}nodes, that's a LARGE system?

    I think most reasonable people who consider a cluster of
    8 12-processor 8400's to be a `LARGE system'.

}                              But it is ideal for people who think the prices
}of the SMP machines are out of reach (and arbitrarily high, compared to the
}equivalent workstations - or is the workstation's price arbitrarily low?) and 
}need a low-latency, high-bandwidth interconnect. If you (i.e., DEC marketing) 
}really think that there has not been much demand for that, then my "brainless"
}comment _does_ apply.

    It is not at all unusual to see a product like this first available
    for the highest-end systems and work down the product line.  The
    obviously can't do everything at once, so it only makes sense to
    first do it where they are going to best recoup their R+D dollars.
    If Memory Channel wins DEC *one* big N*8x00 server sale, how many
    As255 sales would it have to win them to equal that?

}Regarding the remark on console code, documentation, etc., I don't understand 
}that. Are you telling me that the PCI is so different on all those PCI-based
}systems that you have to re-write and re-qualify the driver in every single
}case? Then industrial software development is in an even worse state than
}academic software development, and I already think the latter is almost
}beneath contempt.

    What would be beneath contempt is qualifying a product on a
    system on which it has not been thoroughly tested.

    There are occasions when you want to be conservative (e.g.,
    a bank - they want to be damn sure it works right), and there
    are times you want to be looser (e.g., a student lab, heck,
    if it doesn't work, it just gives you more project ideas :)

    If you are so all fired up about memory-channel, buy it and
    install it in your AlphaStations -- probably it works, maybe
    it doesn't.

    As it happens we were looking at memory channel (and oracle
    parallel server) for a pair of AlphaServer 1000A's a few
    months back -- at that time it was not even qualified for
    the 1000A -- we could have decided to go with it -- DEC
    would certainly have sold it to us -- but we didn't.  On
    the other hand, the config we ARE using[1] isn't DEC-qualified
    either (at least it wasn't at the time), but we felt from
    our own investigation that it would work and if not we had
    a reasonable fallback position.

------------
[1] DECsafe shared-scsi with 5 KZPSA's in each system.

