In article <y43f16zghk.fsf@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de>,
 Jan Vorbrueggen <jan@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de> writes:

:Regarding the remark on console code, documentation, etc., I don't understand 
:that. Are you telling me that the PCI is so different on all those PCI-based
:systems that you have to re-write and re-qualify the driver in every single
:case? Then industrial software development is in an even worse state than
:academic software development, and I already think the latter is almost
:beneath contempt.

  Digital qualifies peripherals and devices in a large number of systems
  and system configurations -- we do this to make sure that the device
  will work as expected, and to try to find (and fix) problems (in the
  supported configurations) before a customer might see them...

  As for your PCI and software quality comments, I can only assume you
  have never become, uh, `familiar', with the internal system architectures
  used for various processors including the VAX and Alpha series processors.
  (This level of familiarity something most sane folk studiously avoid, and
  particularly if staying sane is a goal... :-)

  That the PCI or Q-bus or UNIBUS or XMI or SBI or TURBOchannel or CMI or
  MASSBUS is within itself consistent across more than one implementation
  is one thing, but one will come to realize that the internal system I/O
  architecture and even some low-level characteristics of the system --
  things as simple as the number of busses, the particular physical memory
  mapping, the I/O capabilities of the implementation, etc. -- can and often
  do differ -- to varying degrees -- across various platform "families".

  Some examples:

	o Folks that have worked with drivers and modules for the Q-bus
	  will certainly remember that the creation of faster processors
	  -- processors that pushed the Q-bus specifications -- uncovered
	  various timing problems latent in certain Q-bus widgets, and
	  bugs in certain drivers.  (One of my KA630 drivers failed when
	  it was moved to KA650.)  And folks will remember that most of
	  Q-bus VAX processors had DMA scatter-gather capabilities, but
	  some didn't.

	o Folks that have performed testing and integration of SCSI devices
          will understand the vagaries a visceral level -- that a particular
	  SCSI device might work correctly on one SCSI implementation has
	  little (or no) bearing on its expected operation in another SCSI
	  implementation.  (The PCI is rather better than SCSI here, but
	  similar testing and integration steps are applicable.)  This
	  newsgroup is *littered* with folks that have seen this.

	o Folks that have worked with BI and XMI and even the Q-bus will
	  realize that the memory mapping differs on the same processor,
	  when differing amounts of physical memory are installed.

   Additional examples of why `integration and testing' is needed are
   available on request.  :-)

  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Stephen 'Hoff' Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com
