: I did not see the original remark, but while debugging PCI device drivers
: to run on Alpha VMS I have seen very unexpected behaviour differences
: between various models based on console code.

I also didn't see the original remark, but I can't imagine anyone
saying that a rewrite is necessary.  In fact, one of the articles I 
did see said that all that was needed was qualification.

There is a lot of difference between a 8400 that can support up to 144
PCI slots, and a 500/400 that can support four.  The differences aren't
in the PCI slots, the differences are in what the CPU has to do to
communicate with the PCI slots, and vice versa.  It would be nice if
this difference was invisible to software, but it is not.  You really
do want to do some qualification (i.e. testing!) before you ship it,
and each qual costs money, so business decisions have to be made.  Are
you suggesting it should be shipped unqual'd?

BTW one point that could be made is that Digital could do better at
providing more commonality between system models, and I agree with 
that point.  This would reduce the cost of qualification.  But there
are limits to what can be done by any computer vendor, and if you want
a decent range of product offerings (and DEC wants laptop to mainframe!)
there are points at which you decide to have unique system designs.

