G'day one and all,

I have yet to come accross a platform other than an amiga that will read - let 
alone write - amiga floppy disks. Just ask on comp.emulators.misc and you will 
get one or two (!) replies.

As far as Linux is concerned, I suspect what you are referring to is Linux's 
FDISK utility. I think it will be able to recognise an amiga partition on a 
hard disk. Whether Linux itself can support, and do anything useful with, the 
file system is another question altogether.

AFAICR the `problem' is hardware. Amiga's use(d) some dodgy electronics to 
squeeze 880K onto a DSDD floppy and 1.6Mb onto a DSHD floppy. As a previous 
poster states, the best way is to use an interim disk format. DOS FAT works just
fine although I would reccomend formatting the disk on a pukka DOS machine. I 
always got unpredictable results from a disk that was MSDOS formatted on an 
amiga.

Have you thought about getting a null modem cable strung between the two boxes?

