In article <y4vie3irxe.fsf@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de>, Jan Vorbrueggen <jan@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de> writes:
<>Well, DEC has gone into the brainless mode again.
<>
<>Here they are with the best thing since the CI, called the memory channel. All
<>information you get suggests that it will be available at least on
<>workstations, since most of the initial information from DEC was under a HPCN
<>heading, discussing it as a transport for PVM, for instance. Calling a DEC
<>channel partner (as you know, they don't talk to you anymore if you're just a
<>little university with 38000 students) and asking for pricing information on a
<>cluster of four 500/333 and the necessary MC pieces gives nice numbers and no
<>indication of trouble. However, once you try to place an order, they (DEC) are
<>quite astonished that you could even consider such a thing, since somebody has
<>decided that all supported systems must have the string "server" in their
<>designation. After thorough and repeated questioning across two continents,
<>they do grudgingly admit that you're not quite the first to ask for such a
<>configuration. 
<>
<>Hey DEC, you haven't got a foot left from previous experiments! So where did
<>you shoot yourself this time? Not yet the brainstem...?
<>
<>This is really ridiculous. To say nothing of the missing support for VMS.
<>
<>So a little initial order with a possible much larger follow-on goes to a
<>technically inferior competitor.

Wow - this seems a little harsh.

Memory Channel hardware became available in Spring this year, and OpenVMS will
support it in the first possible major release - V7.1 - later this year.

OpenVMS has had excellent interconnect support for many years, so the urgency
to support Memory Channel was considerably less than for Digital Unix.
It made good use of resources to have the Digital Unix engineering
group do the initial support (with its associated hardware design and debug
efforts). Meanwhile, the OpenVMS engineering group had many other major
projects to work on (64 bit support, for example). The OpenVMS implementation
of Memory Channel leverages heavily on the excellent work done by the Digital
Unix group.

Regarding your comments on workstations: there simply has not been 
much demand for Memory Channel support on these systems. Memory Channel was
initially targeted at providing a high performance interconnect for large
systems. Support starts at the AlphaServer 8000 series systems, and
works its way down the range to the AlphaServer 1000 series. There is
nothing to stop Memory Channel working on smaller systems (including
workstations) and if there is demand for this it can be done. Memory
Channel has been successfully run on several workstations in our labs. It's
a matter of stuff like console support, testing, documentation etc.

Nick Carr
OpenVMS Product Management
Digital Equipment Corporation



