In article <rreineri.841245049@isdnlin>, rreineri@isdnlin.mtsu.edu (Robert J. Reineri) writes:
:...DEC 90TL term servers do not provide full modem control. 
:I have been told that a 90 TM term server will do this, but before I order 
:such a unit, I thought I'd pose this question to the net.

   Limited modem control (DSR/DTR) is available on the DECserver
   90 TL, and this is often enough for most modem configurations.
   (Full modem control is often unnecessary for most modem configs.)

:On the back of the Alpha there are three ports that the installation manual
:refers to as COM1 COM2 and a parallel port. The COM ports are 9 pin RS-232
:connectors.

   Off the top, I don't know the specific device names used, and in
   the case of some systems such as this, the particular name can be
   affected by the choice of console  -- while it isn't a pretty
   solution, I usually plug a terminal into the port and try to log
   in, and look.  If I can log in, SHOW TERMINAL will show the port. 
   If I can't, I look at the serial line settings such as the baud
   rate, and I also try to see if a process is started up when I try
   to log in.  (Default is 9600 baud, and I'd leave it there.)

   If memory serves, if >>> set console graphics, then the workstation
   console window is OPA0:, COM1 is OPA1: (or TTB0:, depending on the
   release), and COM2 is TTA0:.   If >>> set console serial, then COM1
   is OPA0:, COM2 is TTA0:, and there is no name for the workstation
   console display.

:Please email any responses to rreineri@isdnlin.mtsu.edu

   Ask here, get an answer here.

