I've just finished (?) a new version of my PUTR file transfer program for
MS-DOS, which knows how to read, write, and initialize OS/8 and RT-11 volumes,
and read RSTS volumes (both pre- and post- V8.0), contained in DOS image files,
floppy disks (real RX23, RX26, RX33, RX50, "RX52" (DS RX50), and 720KB
(non-DEC) disks;  RX01, RX02 5.25" workalike disks;  or real RX01 disks with
an 8" drive), or TU58 cartridge tape drives connected through a PC COM port.
PUTR can format all supported floppy disk types.  It also knows how to write
primary and secondary bootstraps on RT-11 volumes, if they contain the proper
driver files, so it can be used to build boot disks for Pros and RQDXs.

Improvements over previous versions:

** Many bugs fixed (including the problem with BOOT on RT-11 volumes).
** Internally reorganized for greater robustness, the source code is no
   longer embarrassing!
** MOUNT auto-detects device types and file systems.
** FORMAT optionally initializes a blank directory structure.
** COPY/DEV/FILE makes image copies of entire volumes, either between volumes
   or to or from a file.  COPY/DEV/BINARY is the same but makes a
   sector-by-sector copy.  MOUNT /FOREIGN has been added to allow this with
   unsupported file systems.
** RX26 (2.88MB) support debugged now that I have a drive (still untested with
   real PDP-11 media, I know of no standard config for an RX23/RX26 on an 11).
** RX33 (1.2MB) support tested now that I have an RX33 on my RQDX3.
** Command files and initialization file.
** MORE processing may be disabled via a SET command.
** Default transfer type for COPY (ASCII or BINARY) may be set via a SET cmd.
** Knows how to drive TG43 signal for 8" disks on Micro Solutions FDCs.

PUTR is available for anonymous FTP from FTP.DBIT.COM (199.181.141.53) in
/pub/putr.  The doc file is also visible on http://www.dbit.com.

As always, please make backups of any important data before trying PUTR.
It is believed 100% safe but I can't test against all possible configurations
or data.

John Wilson
D Bit



