Alpha: Not Just For Digital Any More

(c) June 1996 by Terry C. Shannon, Publisher, Shannon Knows DEC

Spearheading an effort to expand the Alpha franchise, DEC and Samsung
Electronics Co., Ltd. announced an agreement to develop and support a high
volume market for products based on DEC�s 64-bit architecture. The
agreement, announced on June 18, licenses the $21B Korean semiconductor
producer as an alternate source manufacturer of current and future Alpha
implementations. In addition, Samsung acquires the rights to develop,
market, sell, and distribute Alpha CPUs on a worldwide basis. Samsung also
gains access and distribution rights to DEC�s FX!32 software translation
technology.

Secret Facts Revealed

DEC and Samsung have a common objective: success in the post Pentium Pro
era. Such success involves establishing a RISC architecture as a viable
alternative to Intel. The Alpha architecture features all the right stuff
to ensure its success; the question to date has hinged on the pricing side
of the equation. Simply stated, the Most Likely Intel alternative has to
emulate the slogan of Avis Rent-A-Car: �We Try Harder.�

DEC and Samsung have the technological wherewithal to try harder. Indeed,
the current game plan calls for DEC�or one of its partners�to offer Alpha/
WNT PCs at the $2.5-$3K price point one year from now. Given projections
for Alpha PC system budget costs in mid-1997, this seems to be an
attainable goal. 

While Samsung cannot exploit the low-cost 21164PC chip being developed by
DEC and Mitsubishi, there�s no reason why the Korean firm can�t develop
its own Alpha econochip. Such a development appears to be an integral part
of the agreement. Indeed, price-and-performance vis-a-vis Intel remain the
only unanswered questions at this juncture.

See Me, Feel Me, Touch Me...

The new pact with Samsung may help address a longstanding problem with
Alpha: the so-called Stealth marketing syndrome. It is ironic that
Digital, a company who has elected to base its entire corporate strategy
on a computer architecture, has been woefully remiss in articulating the
features and benefits of this architecture to the marketplace. Intel has
done an excellent job garnering mindshare and market share; even the
beleaguered MIPs and PowerPC consortia attempt to maintain a presence in
the hearts and minds of customers. The same cannot be said of Digital
Semiconductor. Asked point-blank, DEC VP Charlie Christ claimed that the
firm would address the �Stealth marketing� issue. Time will tell.

Fab Fulfillment Not the Focus

While Digital Semiconductor would benefit from increased utilization of
the FAB6 plant in Hudson, Samsung plans to manufacture Alpha
microprocessors in its own facilities. Accordingly, the Samsung alliance
fails to address the fill-the-fab issue that continues to render FAB6 a
showcase cost center. Nevertheless, the establishment of a viable second
source for Alpha is critical to the architecture's credibility and
acceptance among first-tier vendors.

Rival vendors contemplating the use of Alpha technology would be less
reluctant to buy chips from a third party than from Digital itself.
Moreover, the existence of geographically dispersed production sites
extends a new level of disaster-tolerance to the Alpha program. If Hudson
is plagued by earthquakes or if North Korea�s �Dear Leader� decides to
embark upon a military misadventure on the Korean peninsula, Alpha
production can continue unimpeded via a mutual aid pact between DEC and
Samsung. 

Search Renewed

The search for a FAB6 partner continues, but Charlie Christ maintains that
increased demand for Alpha chips�which will be spurred by near-term price
cuts on EV5 and EV56 parts�and ramping StrongARM and PCI chip production
levels render break-even an attainable goal even without such a
partnership. A laudable goal, but we shall see. 

