Recently I tried to install a couple of new network cards; 3COM EISA
Ethernet III 579-Twisted Pair to be exact. Initially the EISA config
set them up -- but put one of the cards' IRQs as the same as an
exisiting COM2: port (IRQ3).

I tried going into the EISA config software to change this, but there
is a deeper, apparently hardware related, problem here

When they are plugged in, the system not only doesn't complain that
there are new boards in it, it doesn't care whether existing boards
are moved around (from one slot to another)! This is *not* how an EISA
machine is supposed to work as far as I know.

By the way:
I tried moving boards around to figure out whether 
a) the netcards were bad, or
b) the slots I had plugged them into were dead

Since the video (VGA-ISA card) continued to work when placed into one
of the slots where a new netcard had been, it was a safe bet that a
bad slot wasn't the problem.

The only other ugly variable in this mess is the VGA card(s). None of
them seem to work very well. All have to be set to use IRQ2 in order
to work at all. None of the cards tried give me a feeling of comfort
when we continue to boot the system under UNIX instead of the EISA
config disk.

(The IRQ3 based COM2/ tty2A no longer works either -- even though the
Ethernet cards have been removed)

Any help in how to get the machine to recognize it's constituent parts
would be *greatly* appreciated.

