Hi everybody,

My problem: VAXStation 3500 crashes right after boot after upgrade with
            new CMD CQD-220 Q-Bus SCSI Controller and 8MB additional memory.

Environment: VAXStation 3500, VMS 5.5-2, Q-Bus. 
             Console firmware:        KA650-B  V1.2/0123
             CMD controller firmware: SCSI UTILITY PROGRAM V2.0
                                      ADDITIONAL UTILITIES, REV BLA-24
                                                            SN=21195
             SCSI DISK: CONNER CP3500 (510MB) but same behaviour with
                        IBM DPES 31080 (1,08GB)

                Q-Bus slot assignment from right to left:
----------------------------------------------------------------------
         Old, working configuration,      New, crashing configuration

slot 0:  CPU board                        CPU board
slot 1:  8MB Memory board                 8MB Memory board
slot 2:  8MB Memory board                 8MB Memory board
slot 3:  DELQA Ethernet Controller        8MB Memory board
slot 4:  VCB02 Graphics Board part1       DELQA Ethernet Controller
slot 5:  VCB02 Graphics Board part2       VCB02 Graphics Board part1
slot 6:  TQK70                            VCB02 Graphics Board part2
slot 7:  KDA50 part1, CSR=772150          TQK70
slot 8:  KDA50 part2                      KDA50 part1, CSR=760334
slot 9:  DHV11, CSR=760440, VEC=???       KDA50 part2
                                         
slot10:                                   CMD CQD-220 SCSI Controller,
                                                      CSR=772150
slot11:                                   DHV11, CSR=760500, VEC=310
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The only CSR addresses which were changed from the old to the new config-
uration are the CSR Address of the KDA50 (from 772150 to 760334 which is
DUBx and I can boot from DUB0) and of the DHV11 from 760440 to 760500.
The VECTOR of the DHV11 was set to 310.

Failure description: Machine starts booting from DUA0 (SCSI ID=0) without
problems. About 15 seconds after the line 
"%SYS-I-STARTUP, VMS Startup beginning" (or so), it looks as if the software
would initialize or reset all Q-Bus cards (the green LEDs on some cards
are flashing). The green LED on the CMD SCSI Controller, though, either stays
off forever (boot hangs) or goes on again after some seconds but the disk
hangs as well. 

The same happens when booting from DUB0 (the old, unreliable RA70 disk
which I want to get rid of.)

The only way to boot from the SCSI disk is to start the controller's
firmware and scan the SCSI bus (have't checked if SCSI bus scanning
is really required, maybe starting the firmware is sufficient). 
For that purpose, a serial console rather than the graphics console 
(VCB02) must be used because the firmware program apparently does not 
run on the graphics console (can't see any output or just gets stuck). 
After that, the machine boots up but is still unreliable (I had a crash
every now and then with the disk getting stuck).

Things I've already checked and tried without success:

- SCSI termination & cabling
- used another SCSI disk with the same result (IBM DPES 31080 rather 
  than CONNER CP3500)
- changed SCSI controller parameters SCSI Reset=OFF, DENSITY Mode=OFF
  in controller firmware
- set DISCONNECTION = OFF for SCSI ID#0 in via controller firmware
- switch from SYNCHRONOUS to ASYNCHRONOUS mode for SCSI ID#0 via 
  controller firmware
- run TEST DEVICE in controller firmware: No problems.
- removed DHV11
- removed KDA50 and put CMD SCSI Controller in its place.
- ran with only 16MB by removing memory interconnect plug from memory
  board.

REMARK: Both SCSI disks were previously connected to an ADAPTEC SCSI
        controller in a PC. They were not low-level formatted on the
        VAX. Just connected and initialized. The system was copied
        with
          BACKUP/IMAGE/VERIFY DUB0: DUA0:/IGNORE=(INTERLOCK,NOBACKUP)

I don't think that it's an SCSI problem as I've already checked all relevant
SCSI parameters. I suspect that it's some sort of incompatibility between
either the CMD Controller with my VAX firmware or some VMS driver or some 
sort of CSR/VECT interference.

I'm stuck now. Any ideas anyone what else I could try or what the reason
could be?

Thanks -- Tom

P.S: The command sequence I use to start the controller firmware is:

                u
                i
                d/p/w 20001f40 20
                d/l 20088008 80000002
                d/w 2000146a a72e
                d * 100
                s 400

      (I almost know it by heart now :-(

