: > . APPEND operations don't work quite right, and might leave behind holes in
: >   files.
: This is, It seems, a side effect of porting unix-brain-death to VMS... If
: RMS appends data to a file, it can and does leave holes in the file; but it
: does not matter.
: 
We use RMS for file i/o.

So everybody who is using APPEND operation in C-Kermit has no complaints?
Or is nobody using them?  (LOG blah APPEND, SET FILE COLLISION APPEND,
OPEN APPEND, ...)

: >If you have any other complaints about VMS C-Kermit, express them now --
: >preferably accompanied by helpful suggestions or offers of help.
: 
: Can something be done to make it less of a absolute performance pig
: durring recieves? Systems are near single user... Even running at low
: ( 1 or 2 ) priority kermit is pretty immune to data loss.
: 
This is exactly the kind of thing that needs to be looked at by VMS experts
(unlike me).  My best information on this topic is that behavior you are
referring to occurs only when using serial ports that are not buffered (are
any VAX/Alpha serial ports buffered???) and that do not have the DMA
characteristic (SET TERM blah/PERM/DMA).  As it says in the VMS C-Kermit
"beware file", CKVKER.BWR:

"On some slower VAX models with built-in serial ports, such as the VAXstation
3100 or MicroVAX-II, receiving files on serial ports at (say) 19200 bps
results in high CPU utilization, slowing down the system for other processes.
This is partially because VMS C-Kermit's serial i/o routines need to be
restructured along the same lines as the network ones (nonblocking buffered
reads), but also because on certain systems, such as the VS3100, serial
ports interrupt the CPU every time a character arrives.  Most VMS systems
nowadays, however, support either DMA for serial port i/o, or have their
users coming in through terminal servers."

If any VMS programmers want to take a shot at restructuring C-Kermit's
serial i/o, now would be a good time.

- Frank



