edo@umble.cis.ohio-state.edu (Eric Osborne) writes:

> ...NT video driver for the Appian video board in the DECpc 433W...

I believe the last version out was for NT2.1/Win3.1.  The drivers used to be 
available on DEC's ftp site.  Some URL's for you to look at:

 http://www.pc.digital.com
 ftp://ftp.digital.com/PUB/DEC
 ftp://ftp.digital.com/PUB/DEC/PC-Software-Library

Look for 433, pc433, RGDI, or appian to get at the video stuff for DOS/Win31/NT, 
and download from there.  Appian, developer of the card/software, called the 
thing "Rendition" (RGDI).  It is unlikely that those NT2.1 drivers will work, 
but who knows <g>.  

I'm using my pc433W with Win95 and running 1280x1024x256 on the VR320 19" color 
monitor, with the Win31 RDGI real mode drivers.  Works like a charm, very solid 
and very stable.  Since I'd like to move to NT, I'd be interested in hearing 
about your success (or lack thereof) when you get to the end of the trail on 
this thing.

> ...recently upgraded mine to a Pentium 83 overdrive, and performance is OK, 

Did you choose the P83 after deciding it would be a faster/better deal than the 
DX4/100 Overdrive?  Appreciate your thoughts on that as I'm about to commit to 
upgrading the 486dx33 in mine.

> On a related note, the slot that the Appian board plugs into looks
> suspiciously like a standard ISA slot in the supposedly "slotless" 433w.

Yep, it is a standard ISA slot, although you might have a difficult time putting 
a replacement board in the same case and still get a monitor cable connected. If 
you've got a solution for that, What are your plans for handling that?

The VR320 monitor (if that is the one you have) is a fixed frequency monitor and 
will not work with most standard video cards.  But there are some places that 
make/sell video cards that *will* support the fixed freq monitors with native 
drivers for just about all OS's, including NT4.

