In article <4uvi4o$6b8@crl4.crl.com>, Karl <Karl@crl.com> writes:
|>The ads for Digital's Alpha's sound so attractive.  If we can afford it, 
|>should we get a DEC Alpha 8400 with 12 CPU's and two gigabytes RAM?  
|>Does it come with Digital Unix and at a reasonable license fee for a 
|>large number of users?  Are Digital systems true parallel processors, or 
|>is their claim of "synchronized multi-processing" misleading us?  Are 
|>there more powerful systems also at a reasonable price?  It seems to be 
|>that if Alpha is a true parallel processing system, unlimited capacity 
|>can come about by simply connecting as many Alpha CPU's and systems  
|>together as needed.

There are lots of ways of defining parallel processing systems; in the case of
the Alphas they're better thought of as "symmetric multiprocessor" systems that
as "minimally parallel" systems.  In other words a 12-processor system can
handle 12 different things simultaneously with reasonable efficiency (assuming
they aren't all I/O intensive, or all memory intensive; and maybe even then, I
don't know the various bandwidths off-hand); but if you want to parallelize your
code, don't plan on scaling up to a 64 (or 4096, or whatever) processor system.

BTW, I think you're mis-reading what you saw, I believe they refer to them as
"symmetric" rather than "synchronized".  Synchronized implies that there is some
master processor that's controlling all of the others, typical to (at least
some) massively parallel machines; symmetric means that no CPU is in control, no
one CPU handles all of the I/O or all of the interrupts, and this is typical of
modern multiprocessing systems from DEC, Sun, SGI, IBM, etc.

