>Do you have some bizarre idea that PC programs are written in
>assembler or something? Most apps are written in C, and are portable
>to any environment supporting the window calls used. Applications in
>JAVA will not be inherently faster, more portable, or resistant to
>tooth decay. It's just another tokenized programming language, UCSD
>Pascal dressed in a new suit with a bow tie.
>


Do you have some bizarre idea that C with windows calls are portable !!
C on PCs is often not standard itself - eg Turbo C ( at least in earlier
versions was very non-standard ). The Windows calls included in these
C compilers are certainly not standard. Writing code in Java overcomes
these problems. You only need to write the code once. Once 'compiled'
it will run on any platform supporting the Java virtual machine.
Hence you do not need to 
1) Rewrite your PC code to run on a Unix box because the C compiler
on your PC and the C compiler under Unix make different assumptions
about how things are handled by the operating system.
2) Have to have both a PC and one of each of the Unix boxes you wish to port
to in order to compile your code on each system.

Now IMHO although the Unix,VMS, MVS etc market is smaller than the PC desktop
market it is still a very sizeable market. Hence any 'standard' which gets
going which will allow writing code with low development costs to run on all
these machines will make a lot of economic sense.

The end result of this would be the marginalising of the PC only software
market.

I am not saying this will definitely happen - markets are very unpredictable
and some of the best ideas do not come to fruition - however this is a very
real possibility.

David Webb
VMS Systems Manager
Middlesex University



