Alan Frisbie wrote:
> 
> 
> Which of the low-end graphics cards available for the 1000A will
> satisfy this requirement?   It looks like there are four possible
> choices, but VMS support is a big question.   Also, it is unclear
> if these cards are supported on the 1000A.   The Systems and Options
> catalog is unclear, depending on which section you read.   The
> choices are:
> 
>   ATI Mach64 PCI Graphics Accelerator (PB2GA-FB $399)
>   "ATI MACH64 GX ISA 2D gfaphic" (E-Store description)
>

  The PB2GA-FB is a ISA card.  There was a -FA product that was a PCI
  card.
 
>   PowerStorm 3D10 8-plane PCI graphics Accelerator (PB2GA-JA $399)
>   "TRIO64 S3 2MB DRAM PCI card" (E-Store description)
> 
>   PowerStorm 3D30 2D 8-plane PCI graphics Accelerator (PBXGB-AA $795)
>   "2D 8 plane gfx accelerator" (E-Store description)
> 
>   ZLXp-E1 2D 8-plane PCI graphics Accelerator (PBXGA-AA $795)
>   "ZLXp-E1 8 PLANE ADAPTER" (E-Store description)
> 
> Which of these are *really* supported on the AlphaServer 1000A
> under VMS?   For instance, the ATI Mach64 is listed for the
> 1000A, but the resolution is given as 1024x768.   Elsewhere,
> the description of the board lists it as 1280x1024.   Which
> is correct?   The ZPXp-E1 is listed for the 1000A, but only
> for UNIX and Windows NT.   For other machines, it is listed
> with VMS support.   This is very puzzling.
> 

  There are two questions, what works, and what has been qualified as
  a supported option (and passes FCC).

  The PB2GA-FB is the card that the server group uses, as it does not
  consume a PCI slot, and is cheap.  The PCI cards tend to be used by
  the workstation group, who believe that performance is important -
  even for the low-end.

  The ISA version of the Mach64 initially shipped with a bug in the SW that
  prevented 1280x1024 from working (Mach64 cards use multiple implementations
  of both the Mach64 chip - the GX, CX, CT - but also multiple RAMDACs, which
  is what broke the ISA version).  This was recently fixed, and will be in the
  next release of the O/S, and will also be made into a patch kit.

  The ZLXp-E1 is supported by the Open3D product.  The 2D functionality does
  not require a license to run.  This is generally true of anything called a
  "Powerstorm" or ZLXp-*.  The 2D functionality is often rolled into the O/S
  especially for the low-end of these cards.  The S3 Trio64 card does not
  need Open3D however.

> Also, what do the 3D30 and ZLXp-E1 boards give me, beyond what
> the lower-priced boards do?
> 

  3D capable via Open3D (OpenGL, PEX, etc), and performance.

> What is the difference between the 3D30 and the ZLXp-E1?
> 

  The ZLXp-E1 aka TGA aka SFB+ for the PCI.  The 3D30 aka TGA2 is the
  follow on chip.

> For DECwindows scrolling, will either the 3D30 or ZLXp-E1
> significantly outperform the Mach64 and 3D10 boards?
> 

  Not really.  But if you run a 3D wireframe, or anything truly graphics
  intensive - then they will outperform it by a wide margin.  The S3
  and ATI cards are good general purpose cards, for a low price.  The
  PCI implementations will get ~7-9 xmarks.  The 3D30 and ZLXp-E* are
  cutting edge performance starting in the teens for xmarks and getting
  way up into the thirties on some platforms.

> Is DEC's version of the ATI Mach64 board the same as what I can
> buy at my local computer store?   If this board will give me
> adequate scrolling, I am tempted to just buy it locally.
> 

  Yup.  But remember that a lot of these cards use interchangeable parts
  that are not really interchangeable - especially ATI.  VMS and UNIX
  need to do tweaks to the code to handle the init of the cards correctly
  (NT and Intel boxes use the BIOS code to do the init).  So, let the buyer
  beware.

  BTW - the ATI cards are my cards of choice.  Good performance, low price.
  Plus having written a lot of the X11 code for VMS/UNIX for it, I liked it's
  design.  The S3 is another good choice, despite it's design (and I did a
  good bit of that code too).

  If you can burn a PCI slot, use the ATI Mach64 CT PCI card - it's a single
  chip implementation with 1 or 2 mb of DRAM.  Or the S3 Trio64 PCI cards
  (from Dimond or #9).



