[...]
>   The earlist TEAC drive I bought ($180) had everything.  The last TEAC
> I bought ($40) had a motor, head, and PC board.  And that board only
> had an ASPIC chip on it.  Make sense?  The market at work, no rip-off.

The Teac FD55 has gone through a totally rediculous number of revisions (well,
rediculous if you consider that all have the same basic model number).

I've personally come across 4 totally different main logic boards, and have
service data on 3 of them :

a) Control logic is a 48 pin PQFP chip, read/write circuit is a 30 pin
shrinkdip

b) Control logic and read/write are both 48 pin PFQP's (Control is the same as
(a) ?)

c) Read/write same as (b), control logic in larger PQFP, and includes the
buffers/drivers for the cable interface. Track 0 sensor now mounted on logic
PCB.

d) Control and read/write in one even larger PQFP. I think this drive only
existed in 80 track (F,G) versions.

There were a variety of spindle motors used with these drives, and AFAIK types
(a) and (b) only used single-speed (300rpm _or_ 360rpm) motors, while later
drives had a speed control line, and could use switchable-speed motors.

Suffix letters indictated the drive capacity (PC capacity in brackets, although
these drives are used on a variety of machines)

A : Single sided 40 track (180K)
B : Double Sided 40 track (360K standard drive)
E : Single Sided 80 track (not used on PC's, but would be 360K. RX50-compatable
I think)
F : Double sided 80 track (720K, although not commonly used)
G : Double sided 80 track, 360rpm (Standard 1.2Mbyte)

All drives (AFAIK) had optional head load solenoids (those without were called
CSS drives) and some could have door locks, etc.

