In article <4uv8bv$9n7@kannews.ca.newbridge.com>, Paul DeMone <pdemone@tundra.com> writes:
|> When SGI acquired Cray Research an immediate question mark was what
|> would happen with the Alpha 21164 based T3E parallel supercomputer.
|> SGI has stated that it will over time move towards unifying its
|> product line under the MIPS RISC architecture.  Unfortunately DEC
|> is apparently unable or unwilling to acquire the T3E product line as Sun has
|> with Cray's SPARC based commercial
|> systems.
|> 
|> What will happen to the T3E product line?  SGI has been somewhat vague
|> about a potential upgrade of the T3E line with the "shrunk" 0.35 um
|> 21164 (sometimes referred to as the 21164A).  The other question is
|> what options are there for a non-Alpha upgrade roadmap for the T3E?
|> How well does the anticipated 275 MHz MIPS R10K stack up against a 433
|> or 500 MHz 21164A?  Then there is the issue of the imminent announcement
|> of the 21264, next generation Alpha.

Most serious users of supercomputers write fairly portable code
(because they want to scrounge any free time that's going!)  The
Alpha/MIPS/Power/SPARC question isn't really of much interest,
compared with the overall architecture of the machine (e.g. the
type of parallelism).  Provided that SGI can deliver the speed,
I doubt that most users will care which chip is used (or even
notice, in many cases).

Note that all of those have IEEE arithmetic - portability IS a
hassle when going between Cray, IBM 370 and Intel arithmetics.


