In article <4ul1pn$o8p@news.inforamp.net>,
Geoffrey Welsh <crs0794@inforamp.net> wrote:
[transparent floppies]
>I've created one of these using a Shugart SA390 (or perhaps SA400) 5
>1/4" floppy drive.  However, in this case, I suspect that the
>'grinder' wasn't the read/write head but rather some grit that got
>into the felt pad that pushed the disk down onto the head.

Let's think about this for a tick: to make a transparent floppy you need
to grind through the oxide layer (if present) on both sides.  

If the floppy had only one oxide layer, surely it would be under the 
head, not the felt pad?  

Personally, I've never seen a 5.25" floppy that didn't have oxide on both 
sides, even "single sided" ones.  It was pretty much standard practice 
back in my apple][ days to use cheap single-sided diskettes as 
"flippies" simply by cutting an extra write-enable notch in the jacket.  
(The disk][ didn't use the index hole.)

