>
>Note of course that 8" floppy drive spindle motors are *much* bigger than
>5.25" floppy drive spindle motors, presumably coz you need more torque to
>spin an 8" floppy in its jacket.  That makes stopping and starting the
>spindle rather less of an option than on the relatively tiny motors found
>on 5.25" drives.  

	Also, while *all* the 5.25" floppy drives had DC powered motors, the
8" floppy drives usually had AC powered synchronous motors, which were
designed for 24 hours/day operation.  (Yes, the PerSci drives had DC motors,
and I believe that the later 1/2 height 8" drives also had DC motors
(pancake design, similar to later 5.25" half-height floppy drives.


