motor control solid-state relay to the box so all drives spun up whenever
any one of them was accessed (pretty much the same as the behavior of the
bank of 5.25" drives).  I had to wire-wrap a modification to the controller,
which could be set up as either a 5.25" controller or an 8" one, but four of
whichever.  My modification allowed a mix of types, with a dip switch to
select which drives were to be treated as 8" drives.

	Anyway, it worked fine as long as I didn't try to boot from an 8"
floppy.  However, when I tried to boot from one, the system waited for a
floppy to be inserted before turning on the motor, and the drive required at
least two revolutions of the floppy before it would agree that it had a disk
inserted. (It counted index holes to determine ready status :-) Normally,
when boot was from the 5.25" drives, there was no problem, because that boot
process would turn the motors on the 8" drives on as well, but if you
changed disks while it was in a long processing loop, it would never see the
disks as being present untill the program failed and returned things to the
OS.

	So -- the wonderful addition of the 8" floppy motor control fell
victim to the sophistication of the drive's logic.

