organization: University of Cambridge, England
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


eric@goonsquad.spies.com (Eric Smith) writes:

>> PDP 11/04 connect terminal is RS232 20mA current loop.

>EIA-232 and 20mA current loop are two completely different things.

Well, they're not _completely different_ - they're both serial 
interfaces, and in this case, the bytes are sent in the same way. 

>Some DEC equipment can be strapped for either EIA-232 or current loop, but
>I don't think the 11/04 console port can.  Perhaps someone with more 11/04
>experience can confirm or refute this.

From what I recall, the 11/04 console port is a normal DL11-W card, like 
the one in the 11/34 (It's the 11/05 that has the 20mA-only port on the 
CPU board). Now, that DL11-W came in 2 versions, current loop only, and 
current loop _and RS232_. 

Trace the console cable (it'll probably end in a flat white 8-pin 
mate-n-lock connector) to the card inside the machine (I forget the 
M-number). If that card has some 1488 and 1489 chips on it (and it's 
likely to do so), then use the standard DEC serial cable (the wirelist of 
which is on the web somewhere, probably on one of the PDP8 pages), and 
hook up a normal terminal/PC.

If you don't have the 1488/1489 chips then either buy/build a converter, 
or get back to me and I'll look up exactly what has to be added to a 
DL11-W to give it the RS232 facilities.


>Cheers,
>Eric

