Andrew Gallatin <gallatin@diego.isds.duke.edu> wrote:
| These numbers are from Digital UNIX -- you're probably going to see
| lower number under Linux or NetBSD.

Very, very doubtful.  While Digital Unix has certain things going for
it (Advfs, SMP, better overall support, h/w diagnostics, etc.), raw
efficiency isn't necessarily one of them.  Linux and NetBSD aren't
obligated to support all the cruft that causes the Digital Unix
bloat/performance loss, not to mention that both consistently
outperform the commercial OSes on any given platform being
tested. (Linux/Sparc is just as stable and quite a bit more efficient
than both SunOS 4 and 5, according to performance/stability tests, and
I'd wager NetBSD isn't doing too shabby either).  Having said that,
they are still working on fully optimizing gcc for the alpha and even
without that, Linux/Alpha is performing quite well.  Please don't
start an OS war because of this.  I'm quite fond of several different
platforms and operating systems, Digital Unix and Linux both happen to
be two of my favourites.

Regards,
Roman



