I'm not intending to start an OS war (BTW Digital UNIX and NetBSD are two
of my favourites.)

The primary advantage to Digital UNIX (at least in terms of raw speed)
are due to its compilers.  (And if you're doing scientific
programming, the Extended Math Library is quite useful).  You may not
see much of an advantage on relatively slow machines like like the
UDB.

On high end machines, you'll notice the difference.  Going from DU3.2C
to 4.0, we've seen a speedup in some of our in-house benchmarks of
about 25% when the executables are compiled using the new DEC C
compiler.  I would venture to guess that this is due to improved 21164
instruction scheduling in the new compilers.

However, as you've correctly pointed out, benchmarks aren't
everything.  The free OSes may be more usable and feel faster on a
smaller or slower system.  I'd also hazzard a guess that with the
money you've saved by running a free OS and not buying the DU base
license, compilers, and math libs you could probably spend enough more
$$ on hardware that you would probably come out ahead.

Cheers,

Drew






