In article <4udnqf$pjh@sloth.swcp.com>, phdewi@swcp.com (Phillip Williams) writes:
> In a auction recently I obtain the following drives. They are
> 9720-850 Sabre Drive by CDC.

  I love it when people buy things they have no ideas about 8-)

> They're  24"LX8.5"WX5"H. On the top of
> each unit theres 2 rocker switches. On the back theres 2 more rocker 
> switches with 4 60 pin male connectors and 2 26 pin male connectors. I 
> also got a couple of Hitachi LTD DK815-5 units. So the questions are

  [assuming you're asking about the CDC units]

> 1. What size are they??

  850MB unformatted, give or take a few. The dash suffix indicates the size -
I think this series goes up to about 1.3GB unformatted.

> 2. Are they RA/RD type drives??

  No, they're SMD-E drives. If they were RA disks they'd have an SDI inter-
face on them. Some vendors sold Sabre drives with an SDI adapter board -
one such example is EMC.

> 3. What type of controller will work with them??

  On what sort of computer? A Q-bus system? Something like an Emulex QD-33
would work and would give you MSCP emulation. The earlier QD-32 can't deal
with the data rate the 850's have - the biggest Sabre they can support is
the -736 version.

  Note that these drives have *lots* of internal switches and jumpers that
need to be set properly to work with a particular controller. You'll need
the manual for the particular revision of the CDC drive (there were quite
a few different logic boards used with different switch settings. Also, the
firmware changed the interpretation of the switches occasionally). 

  If you're lucky, you got the drives with the optional display front panel,
which has an LCD display for indicating status, setting the unit number, etc.
While it's possible to operate a drive without it (many OEM configurations
ran in that mode) it's very hard to diagnose problems without the display.

  Note that the power supply was also optional - make sure you have that as
well.

> 4. The Hitachi's don't have a powerr connector so do they require 3 phase
>  power or what??

  I don't know.

