Phillip Williams wrote:
> 
> In a auction recently I obtain the following drives. They are
> 9720-850 Sabre Drive by CDC. They're  24"LX8.5"WX5"H. On the top of
> each unit theres 2 rocker switches. On the back theres 2 more rocker
> switches with 4 60 pin male connectors and 2 26 pin male connectors. I
> also got a couple of Hitachi LTD DK815-5 units. So the questions are
> 1. What size are they??
> 2. Are they RA/RD type drives??

From your description of the connectors, I guess the type is: 97200-850
which makes it a SMD type disk with 850 Mb unformatted capacity.
Formatted capacity depends on the controller. There's also the
97201-850 Sabre which is the SCSI version. The SMD interface has a
2 60 pin connectors (1J04 & 1J03) for the A-cable a 26 pin connector
(1J02) for the B-cable.
The SABRE has two sets (1Jxx & 2Jxx) because it has a built-in dual-port
feature, you can switch it between two machines. The two A-connectors
are used for daisy-chaining multiple drives, the last drive in the chain
needs a terminator. Each drive has it's own B-cable. 
 
So:

		 --------------------------------
		|	Controller		 |
		 --------------------------------
		 |     |	  |	      |
                 | ---------   ---------      |
                 | |   |    | |   |     | T   |
	         A A   B    A A   B     A A   B
	        ---------  ---------   --------
		|        | |        |  |       |
		|  Drv   | |   Drv  |  |   Drv |
		-------------------------------


The disk division of CDC became the company IMPRIMIS which was sold to
SEAGATE. SEAGATE's type number for the drive is ST8851J (97200-850) or
ST8851N (97201-850). See:
ftp://ftp.seagate.com/techsuppt/misc/old2new.txt for the type number
conversion scheme, and 
ftp://ftp.seagate.com/techsuppt/smd/st8851j.txt for some data about the
drive.

The SABRE's where the drives we used in the configurations we advised
our customers in the 8" era until we switched to the 3 1/2" Digital
DSP 3105 drives. They are good quality, the only major problem we had
was cooling. You have to make sure there is an airflow possible 
around the drive, we had head-crashes because we stacked them to 
closely. 

> 3. What type of controller will work with them??

A Q-bus SMD controller, for instance QD3x series from Emulex or 
DQxxx series from DILOG. Our last type was QD35, which is a caching
SMD controller for max. 4 drives and has a very good performance.

> 4. The Hitachi's don't have a powerr connector so do they require 3 phase power or what??

Don't know them, sorry.

