I just wanted to point out that DEC's version of the TEAC (the RX33) made
my life difficult by omitting the spring dealie that kicks the floppy out
when you open the door. When you open the door on a TEAC RX33, the floppy
just sits there.

The difficulty with this is that the drive requires the diskette to move
about 1/8" before it will assert Diskette Changed. In short, on a floppy
controller I did I had no way of knowing if the drive was actually spinning
(I kept imagining a pesky user who would wait until the controller thought
the drive was up to speed, open the door, and giggle uncontrollably).
