> file, $ show mem reveals 112 Mbyte RAM, no errors. However, full boot
> stalls in DECNet startup, investigation reveals the DNS$ADVER process
> dies early in startup, and STARTUP process just hangs in LEF state.
> In the DNS$ADVER logfile is the message.
> 
> $  ty DISK$AVMSBUGS:[SYS0.SYSMGR]DNS$ADVER_ERROR.LOG;-1
> Calculated Cache Size - (626688 pages)
> Warning - Cache sizing discrepancy found:
> 
> (when the system boots sucessfully the second line is not there).
> 
> A 96 Mbyte (2*32 and 4*8) configuration boots OK, thats what I'm running
> now.
> 
> Does anyone have any ideas about this weirdness? Are there undocumented
> restrictions on memory configurations in this machine? Is the 3rd
> party RAM suspect? Or is it a DECNET OSI bug. btw OSI version is
> 
> Version = "DECnet/OSI for OpenVMS Version V6.2-ECO04  7-OCT-1995 18:41:22.92"

I had a similar problem with an AlphaStation 200. It is caused by a
DECNET/OSI bug. The workaround is to do a minimum boot and delete
SYS$SYSTEM:DNS$CACHE.*;*. Then shutdown and boot normally and all should
be well.
-- 
	- Jay Olson (jjo@triton.com)
	Triton Software Group Ltd.



