>shoppa@alph02.triumf.ca (Tim Shoppa) writes:

	[ ... ]

>>as replacements for 8"DSDD drives, after all, and many 8" drive systems
>>assumed that the head would load much more quickly than the motor would
>>start turning!  
>
>The other subtlety is that an 8" drive is 'ready' if there's a disk 
>loaded (and other conditions are met), while a 5.25" drive (at least, a 
>PC-clone one) is 'ready' if there's a disk loaded _and_ it's up to speed. 
>So an 8" controller that waits for the drive to be ready before loading 
>the head will never attempt to start a 5.25" if you connect motor on to 
>the head load signal. Don't ask how I discovered this.

	As I remember from examination of the schematics, the "ready" line
on the 8" drives was activated by two full revolutions (two passes of the
index hole), and cleared by opening the drive door.

	I don't remember what was done with the hard-sectored drives, but
there was a one-shot which was used in discriminating between the index hole
and the sector holes.

