I think this is a DECNET/OSI problem - I can't remember the details, as
it came up a couple of years ago, and I can't find the details in my
several hundred DECNET/OSI mails! I also thought it was fixed prior to
DECNET/OSI V6.0. It is related to the size of the cache that the
DNS Clerk tries to use - if the amount of physical memory is increased
it readjusts the cache size, and can run into troubles doing this. My
suggestion would be to rename the DNS cache files - both
DECNET$CDI_CACHE.DAT and DNS$CACHE.nnnnnnnnnn (and hide the
DNS$CACHE.VERSION file) when DECNET/OSI is down and try rebooting - 
DNS Clerk should then create new cache files from scratch and hopefully
be happy.
Sorry to be so vague on this - if I trace the relevant mails I'll come
back with more information. Incidentally DEC know of this problem (it
was mail from DEC that I was trying to find) - they should be able to
advise you.
Regards,
