In article <4u7qk6$3ge@oac2.hsc.uth.tmc.edu> dct@valhalla.mda.uth.tmc.edu (David C. Tuttle) writes:
> We have two Alpha-based "Easy Web Servers" (Universal Desktop Box)
> with Digital RZ26F 1.0GB drives.  It has been proposed to put both
> internal drives on one of the boxes (and put a larger drive in the
> other box).  But is this even possible?  I'm told they'll both fit,
> but I don't see how.  Has anyone done this successfully?

They might physically fit if you take out the floppy.  But I don't know if
the power supply can handle it, and the cabling will be a problem.

The Multia/UDB SCSI on the riser is terminated, and the internal disk drive
isn't.  They somehow manage to get away with this (and the absence of a second
terminator when you don't have external devices) because the internal SCSI
stub length is fairly short.  If you have longer internal cabling you will
need to add a terminator at the end of the internal cable, and you will not be
able to use external SCSI devices (unless you desolder the SCSI termination
resistor packs on the riser).

It is also possible to put a 3.5 inch drive into the Multia/UDB, but you
give up the PCI slot.  I think you need different mounting hardware than is
supplied in the units which are shipped with internal 2.5 inch drives.

It should be much easier to simply add an external SCSI box.  Around here
they go for under $60, although the high-density to low-density SCSI cable
may cost you $30, and an active terminator might add another $30.

Cheers,
Eric



