:  There is a Q-bus to SCSI adapter. 

   Digital offers the KZQSA Q-bus to SCSI and the KFQSA Q-bus to DSSI.
   One will have to go to third-party Q-bus to SCSI adapter, or to an
   HSDxx DSSI-SCSI controller off a KFQSA DSSI adapter, to add SCSI
   (non-CD-ROM) disks to a VAXstation 3500 series system.

: There shouldnt be any special problem using VMS V5.5-2 on a VAXstation
: 3500 from a SCSI disk, but some precautions should be taken. 

   The KZQSA is not supported for magnetic disks under OpenVMS.  The
   controller is supported only for SCSI CD-ROM and tape devices.

: As many have recommended on this newsgroup, and as I recommend as
: well from my experience, use a DEC SCSI disk for the system disk.
:
:  Some of the common problems are disks which do not spin-up on power, and
: firmware problems, which tend to creep up at VMS upgrades.
:
:  I remember a thread about SCSI disks which work with OpenVMS in this forum
: a short time ago, so you can look in the archive of the last two or three
: months for other people's exprience.

   My personnal recommendation is to purchase a disk with the clear
   understanding of what level of vendor support is provided, what level
   of integration and testing is provided, and a clear idea of what tasks
   you are willing and capable of -- do _not_ assume that "SCSI" means
   the device is interchangeable, and do _not_ assume that any particular
   SCSI device is "correctly implemented", nor that it will work in your
   configuration.  (The archives are littered with "my drive doesn't work"
   discussions...)

   SCSI is a collection of "standard" device queries and responses and a
   communications (electrical, in the current SCSI standards) interface. 
   Whether or not a particular combination _works_ is dependent on the
   host software, on the host adapter, on the device implementation, on
   communications loading, on other devices on the bus, and on internal
   device SCSI parameter settings.  Getting a SCSI device to work often
   requires changes to firmware and/or settings, and getting it to work
   both quickly and reliably almost always requires parameter changes.

   There are three levels of SCSI: SCSI-1, SCSI-2, and (work-in-progress)
   SCSI-3.  SCSI-2 supports narrow & wide, differential & single-ended,
   slow & fast, and various combinations of these -- there are likely a
   few other implementation options I've missed here, too.  SCSI-2 is
   a proper superset of the SCSI-1 protocol.

   If you are willing and equipped to perform integration and testing,
   you can purchase drives that have not had these tasks performed.
   If you are not willing or are not able to perform these tasks, you
   will want to purchase the drive from a reputable supplier, and you
   will want to make certain the supplier performs these tasks and is
   willing to support the drive.

  ------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
