I see there's already a reply to this thread - telling you how to
work around a decnet osi bug.

However, there's also a problem with 3000/300s and 32MB
simms.

I just replaced all the memory on 4 3000/300s with Data Ram
32MB simms - giving 256MB per processor. (I got bids of
$674/64MB from Midwest systems - very close to what you can
buy PC memory for!). (The original memory was DEC 8MB simms
for 64MB per processor.)

The 4 3000/300's in question were purchased in 1993 and have the
original system boards.

It turns out that these were Rev D01 boards and **they do not
work with 32MB simms**.

For two of the systems, one could get them to work with 4 of the
32MB simms (128MB). They would boot with 256, but crash as soon
as any load was put on. The other two would not work with 32MB
simms at all. With 256MB, they would crash during boot. With
128MB, they would crash under load.

After some thrashing around, all the system boards have been
swapped and are now Rev R01 boards with firmware version 6.5 and
all the systems are running fine with 256MB memory.  

Assuming you have a hardware service contract, you should ask DEC
to upgrade your system boards to the latest rev. (Or say you need
all the FCOs on your system board.)

Some notes about testing:

In console mode, you can do test mem. This will probably fail in
something called the LLSC test. It will fail at fairly low memory
addresses and it will fail with your existing good memory. (Turns
out this sent me on a wild goose chase for a while!) What you
have to do is >>> set mop off before running the console memory
tests and then >>> set mop on when you're done. Then you will
probably never see a test mem fail, even though the system is
crashing because of memory problems. Apparently the test mem
diagnostics don't stress the system very hard.

As part of this I wound up creating a dump file and getting
remote diagnostics to analyze the dump. Well, with an out of rev
board, the dump is apparently misleading. The reading I got with
8 x 32MB Data Ram simms in at the time of the crash was that the
system contained a total of 8 x 16MB DEC simms and it was simm #3
that caused the crash. Of course, this was bogus!

I guess I have mixed feelings about this whole episode. DEC was
pretty good about swapping the boards once they were convinced it
was the problem. But it took a lot of convincing. (It's taken me
two weeks to get this working from when the memory order
arrived.) How come they don't have in their database that you
need a certain rev to work with 32MB simms? Apparently the only
thing the field service folks were able to find was an article
about problems with Samsung memory. I also contacted DataRam.
They too, were unaware of any minimum rev required to use their
simms.

