In article <htITg4a.howesc@delphi.com>,
Chester Howes  <howesc@delphi.com> wrote:
>Hello DEC users and knowlegeable folks.
> 
>I have just been GIVEN a DEC VAXStation 3100 M38 system.
>My benefactor says he found it in a dumpster > 1 year ago.
> 
>It includes the CPU, model WS42A
>            Monitor, model VR260-AA
>  and a tape drive?, model TK50Z-GA

    Yes a CompacTape drive that holds 95MB per tape.

> 
>The CPU has a floppy, SCSI HD, and two 'DATARAM' boards.
>A 'dummy' plug is plugged into the AUI port.
> 
>The only obvious fault I see is on the monitor cable block screwed onto
>the monitor's back.  This block appears to have an rj-xx kybd socket,
>a mouse port, and a shielded cable to a BNC to the monitor input.
>The shield on this wire is broken at the block.  Probably the video
>will be crappy until this gets fixed.

   No, it should probably be ok.  The cables were just crappy and the
plastic would easily come out of the block if bent enough.  I had one
in that condition for years and it continued to work fine.  Just make sure 
none of the internal cables are broken.

> 
>Will anyone please tell me what I have, what OS it can use, and
>how I should go about testing it and trying to bring it up?  What type
>of mouse will I need?

   You've pretty well identified everything.  The serial ports are Modified
Modular Jacks (MMJ) which are 6-wire rj-11-like connector but with the
key offset to one side.  Adaptors (mmj to rs232) are available from computer
dealers.  The VS3100-38 can run VMS, Ultrix or Netbsd (port in progress)..
You'll need either a VSXXX-AA (dec puck mouse) or a VSXXX-GA (a logitec
oem) mouse.

   Power it in and see if it completes all of it's internal tests.  It will
count down from F to 1.  Each hex number represents a different test.  IF
you get a ?? error, that indicates a hardware problem.  A single ? is a
not so serious error and is expected with some configurations (i.e. an
empty external SCSI bus).  If you get to the >>> prompt, try a SHOW DEVICE
to see if the peripherals are identified.  Typing a "B" will have it try
and boot off the preset boot device, which may be either the disk you have or
ESA0, which is the ethernet device.  A SHOW BOOT will show you.  If it's 
set to ESA0, you can put the boot device after the boot command, e.g.
BOOT DKA100.

--
Vance Haemmerle
vance@alumni.caltech.edu
my VS3100/38 page:  http://condor.lpl.arizona.edu/~vance/toyvax.html





