In article <x06875eyrl.fsf@avanse.nhh.no>,
Tom Ivar Helbekkmo  <tih@avanse.nhh.no> wrote:

| > NCs are more like the rebirth of commercial timesharing, where you
| > pay by the minute, and if you're really unlucky you pay both for
| > connect time AND for CPU time...
| 
| Yup.  We're seeing the precursor of a return to the idea of the
| terminal accessing centralized time sharing systems, and it's a good
| thing, IMHO.

For the vendors, sure. For the user who pays by the resource, no. The PC
Doesn't cost more if you use it (in any significant way), you can let
the family share it and not pay more, you can be sure that your data is
not seen by anyone else but you.

*You* control the software, contrary to thinking in Redmond there's no
legal requirement to upgrade regularly if what you use works for you. My
wife described a certain online software as "easy to use, you click on
anything and it downloads new software." When you don't have local
storage you not only have to wait for the download, you have to take
whatever version you get. That's neat if you collect versions, but if
you want to count on having to day what worked yesterday, you're out of
luck.

I think the NewTerminal will appeal to a certain segment of the
population, but I'm less convinced that that segment has or would have a
computer... ever. It appeals to people who watch TV a lot and want to
play games. After all the BS about "computing for the masses" is said,
it will be primarily an entertainment channel, just as TV has become,
just as the Internet has become, just as usenet has become.

The PC will be a tool for people who primarily want to use a computer,
and who view communications as one facet of that rather than a primary
use. The NewTerminal folks will not want to pay to save the things they
do, will not want to pay to have the kids use it, etc.

The solution to computing for the poor is not a new toy, it's better
access to computers. The folks who can't afford the hardware can't
afford the connect time either, and many of them will buy a terminal
only to find out they can't afford to use it, wasting some of their
limited resources to enrich corporations.

The real benefit will be to the people who can afford computers, but who
just want to play games, or browse the net. They can afford to use it
enough to get some benefit, it comes out of disposable income rather
any part of the necessities budget.

I don't think this will affect the PC market in any major way, I think
it will compete with game systems and movies much more. Oh, and just
like game systems there will *still* be upgrades to more bits, better
chips, more resolution, fancier sounds, etc. As long as there's money to
make by convincing people what they have isn't good enough, the upgrade
race will continue.
