Coast Guard personnel sometimes use various PCs and Macintoshes on
board ship, in addition to the 'official' systems.  In all cases,
they noticed that the disk drives on these systems had a shorter
lifetime than the same systems located on shore.  Somebody finally
tracked down the probable cause as gyroscopic effects.

Ever play with a toy gyroscope?  Ever try to twist it while
it's spinning?

Ships constantly rock (side-to-side and front-to-back) depending
on what kinds of seas they're sailing through).  Even when moored,
they usually move a bit.  The disk platter acts as a gyroscope.
When the ship rocks, the platters try to resist and twist laterally,
putting 'sideways' forces on the bearings.  They aren't designed
to resist force in this direction, and they wear out faster than
if the drive sits relatively motionless as it does in most offices.
Even laptops don't get rocked about as much as ship-board computers.

They now accept that disk drives will have only a 1 year lifetime
on board ships.  It's probably cheaper now to replace the drives
regularly than to try to design and build special drives that
would resist the gyroscopic forces.

-- 
