I seem to recall it was an option on the Tandon as well - there's an unused
connector on the back of the logic board that the schematic implies is 
for such a solenoid. The drives in my XT do not have the solenoid, though.

The drive I got for my TRS-80 model 1 (in a Tandy case - a Teac? 
full-height mechanism) does have a head load solenoid. So, I think do the 
2/3 height (!) Canon drives in my RML380Z.


>>	I suspect that it was the relative difficulty of putting a reliable
>>head-load mechanism in a half-height drive which started the trend to no
>>head-load mechanisms.

>Some 1.44Mbyte TEAC's have head-load as an option.  They were designed

Not just 1.2 Mbyte ones - the FD-55's have the head load as an option on all
models - the 360K 'B' and the 720K 'F' as well as the 1.2Mbytes 'G'. In 
fact, I've never seen such a drive _without_ the head load solenoid, 
although according to the manual, they do exist - called the CSS (any 
ideas ?) version.

>as replacements for 8"DSDD drives, after all, and many 8" drive systems
>assumed that the head would load much more quickly than the motor would
>start turning!  

The other subtlety is that an 8" drive is 'ready' if there's a disk 
loaded (and other conditions are met), while a 5.25" drive (at least, a 
PC-clone one) is 'ready' if there's a disk loaded _and_ it's up to speed. 
So an 8" controller that waits for the drive to be ready before loading 
the head will never attempt to start a 5.25" if you connect motor on to 
the head load signal. Don't ask how I discovered this.


>Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)
