In article <4u2q0d$42d@scotsman.ed.ac.uk>, ajmy@tattoo.ed.ac.uk (A Myles) writes:
:Having previously managed to restore the breath of life into the
:old VS3200 (i.e. re-install VMS4.7A) in my spare time, I've got an
:annoying little problem that I can't find the anwer to. Any help
:will be much appreciated.
<snip>
:[1] Is there any way to get the the console back to full size? This
:    seems to be some figment of VWS half starting. Until it's full
:    size, it's going to be too painful to try to edit startup.com
:    to stop whatever is doing this. The sections on the console and
:    SET/TERMINAL the manual kits don't seem to say anything about
:    resizing the screen, only the page size for type and such.

   An application -- likely VWS -- has started up, but has not completed
   to start up -- on the workstation display.

   Without software assistance, the VAXstation II series console PROM,
   the QDSS controller, and the workstation graphics display has slightly
   more intelligence than the typical electric fence, and is not suited
   for any task requiring ANSI escape sequences.  One is _much_ better
   off attaching a terminal to the console DB9 (see the FAQ for the pinout
   or get a DECconnect DB9-to-MMJ adapter), using a standard terminal, and
   pressing <BREAK> a couple of times to "get the attention" (>>>) of the
   console PROM.

:[2] Should I hunt for and delete any of the files that VWS claims
:    to install? The .stb files seem to be missing from the failed
:    install.

   STB files aren't usually critical to system operations, just to
   program development, and sometimes to system or product
   installations.  (And in this latter case, this can be a result
   of not having enough installed prior to the layered product
   installation, to some (incorrect) by-hand tailoring of the system
   disk, or due to having used the OpenVMS-provided tailoring
   facility to remove some pieces of OpenVMS that are necessary for
   the installation of a particular layered product.

   It's best to reinstall VWS and PURGE.  (It's easy to "miss" and
   delete something "important" the other way...)

   If you are having problems with the TK50 and have enough disk
   space to try this, try to copy the VWS saveset(s) into the root
   directory of the disk (SYS$SYSDEVICE:[000000]), and -- once you
   successfully get the saveset(s) copied in from the TK50 -- run
   the VWS installation from there.

:[3] What exactly is microVMS? I've got a microVMS tape, but can't
:    find the product description sheet and installation instructions.
:    Some things seem to hint at VWS needing microVMS. Yay/nay?

   MicroVMS is VMS, but the salient differences included the packaging:
   MicroVMS is typically a "cut down" version of VMS, and was packaged
   to account for the smaller disks present on the various MicroVAX
   and VAXstation systems of that time.  (Most (all?) MicroVAX and
   VAXstation systems _can_ -- given sufficient available disk space
   -- run "full" VMS.  And this practice was expected on systems that
   were configured in an NI VMScluster -- what was then called an "LAVc".)

  ------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Stephen 'Hoff' Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com
