organization: Victoria University of Wellington
newsgroups: alt.sys.pdp11,comp.sys.dec,vmsnet.pdp-11,alt.sys.pdp8,alt.folklore.computers


In article <4u5f2k$gs5@lyra.csx.cam.ac.uk>,
A.R. Duell <ard12@eng.cam.ac.uk> wrote:
>The other subtlety is that an 8" drive is 'ready' if there's a disk 
>loaded (and other conditions are met), while a 5.25" drive (at least, a 
>PC-clone one) is 'ready' if there's a disk loaded _and_ it's up to speed. 
>So an 8" controller that waits for the drive to be ready before loading 
>the head will never attempt to start a 5.25" if you connect motor on to 
>the head load signal. Don't ask how I discovered this.

Of course an 8" drive spins all the time, whereas a 5.25" (and 3.5") drive
spins only when being accessed.  On the other hand, a 5.25" drive's heads
are loaded all the time there is a floppy in the drive.  The disadvantage
of the 8" approach is that the head loading and unloading all the time is
noisy; the 5.25" on the other hand has a longer initial access time while
the disk spins up.  (Also, many 8" drives unload the heads between seeks,
resulting in lots of "tinkatinkatinka..." noises as the drive does its
stuff, and may actually slow the system down.  My dear departed DSD440
subsystem (Shugart drives, I think) did that; the RX02 I use now isn't so
noisy but that may be because of the sheer size of the drive enclosure...)

What happens if you keep the floppy spinning with the head loaded is
this: you get a transparent floppy.  The heads will dig through the oxide
down to the mylar within a day or two of operation.  (Think about this
when committing vital data to floppies! 8-)

I have seen evidence of this fact in the form of a naked 8" floppy pinned
to the wall of a colleague's office, with a transparent ring around it.
It came (I think) from an IBM Series 1, which used it as a boot floppy.
There was a power failure and subsequent restart, which left the drive no
longer listening to the heads.  The system's "recovery" sequence went:

	Load and spin up disk (from a magazine)
	Load heads
	Read boot sector -- fail
	Unload heads
	Unload disk
	Load disk
	Load heads
	Read boot sector -- fail
	Unload heads
	Unload disk
	Load disk
	Load heads
	Read boot sector -- fail
	Give up.

Spot the missing step....

-- 
