I've just isolated the following code that tickles a bug in DEC's cc compiler.
Hardware: Digital AlphaStation 500/333
Operating System: Digital Unix V4.0 (rev 386)
Compiler: DEC C V5.2-023

The code fragment:

#define PPROTO Py_PROTO
#define Py_PROTO(x) x
static int halfbinop PPROTO((int *, int *, char *, int **,
			    int * (*) PPROTO((int *, int *)), int ));

when compiled with "cc -c PROTO_bug.c" produces the following error message:

cc: Error: PROTO_bug.c, line 3: Missing ")".
static int halfbinop PPROTO((int *, int *, char *, int **,
---------------------^

If I compile with the flag "-oldc", there is no error. The default is to use the
GEM C compiler (-newc), so it looks as though the error is in 
/usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/gemc_cc. The error also occurs if I use the "-migrate" flag
to invoke the GEM C compiler. I can compile this on another machine running
Digital Unix V3.2 and an earlier version of the GEM compiler without error.

Interestingly, if I change either of the PPROTO keywords in the forward
definition of halfbinop to Py_PROTO it compiles without error.

Has anyone come across this one, or got any comments to make (DEC?)?

	Thanks,
