Sven VANLEKE (sven.vanleke@net.alcatel.be) wrote:

: Can any of you help me with the following:

: Is it possible to interconnect 2 asynchronous devices by means of 2 DEC 
: terminal servers (with permanent sessions)? With this I mean, in stead of 
: having 2 asynchromous devices interconnected to each other directly, 
: using those DECservers for it via our local network.

: Scheme: NOW (short distance)
: ----------------------------


: |ASYNC-1|---------------------------------|ASYNC-2|


: Scheme : TO BECOME (longer distance)
: ------------------------------------


:                         Eth
:           port-x         -
: |ASYNC-1|---|DECserver|--|          port-x
:                          |--|DECserver|---|ASYNC-2|
:                          -


: If this is possible, do we need a specific type of server for it and is 
: it there a way to get some parameters for it :-)


DECservers support Reverse LAT connections whereby a /dev/tty can
be mapped to a port on a DECserver so that a printer or modem on
that port can be accessed from Ultrix via that tty line.  So you
would have to patch the two tty lines together inside a common
Ultrix.  I don't know if there are any programs that patch two
tty lines together, or if you would have to cobble that up in C.

It might also be possible to program one port on DECserver #1 to
connect directly to a peer port on DECserver #2 by defining a service
within the DECserver and using the AUTOCONNECT and DEDICATED SERVICE
features.  (I've never tried this.)

Barry Kort



