> Your sense of history is a littly shakey....
> Long before IBM's "winchester" disk drives heads were flying over disks. 
> In fact they had lots of special "ramps" to achieve this.  The heads
> themselves were aerodynamically designed, and had holes to produce the
> desired effects.  This technology dates back to probably the 1950's
(late)
> and the IBM 'RAMAC' disks.

Right ON! The 305 drives (disks 27" in diameter, heads the size of a 50
cent piece, a bicycle chain used to position the heads on the appropriate
disk and a carbon vane air pump to provide the air to make the heads "fly).
The air was delivered to the heads thru a small hose and introduced between
the head and platter through 3 holes approx 1/16 inch in diameter.

These disks followed the drum used on the 650 which was housed in a sealed
enclosure to ensure that the oil mist, used to lubricate the heads in case
of a head crash) did not contaminate the whole system and computer room.

Then came the infamous "toilet paper reader" which stored wide strips of
media in "drums". These strips were "picked" from their storgae location, 
read from or written to and then re-inserted into the storage drum. Didn't
last long - we couldn't make them work reliably.

Then came the 1310 disk drive, used on the 1401 and 1620. Had a hydraulic
head access mechanism which made the device look an sound like an "out of
balance" washingh machine.

Another single platter drive was released called the 1810 which used the
first voice coil head positioning mechanism. This one sounded much like
someone running their thumb along the teeth of a very big comb.

The 2310 followed, then the 2314, 2319 etc., etc. which all used removeable
media.

All of the above drives contained a "head load" mechanism which used a
solenoid to force the heads against their platters once a specific
rotational speed was reached, typically 70% of operational speed. Matter of
fact, most 8 inch floppy drives used a similar mechanism. It was not until
the introduction of 5 1/4 inch floppy drives that the head load mechanism
was removed (probably to save money) and the heads allowed to permanently
contact the disk surface. It was also around this time that media surfaces
were "burnished", or polished, to prevent head wear.

 
> What was new for the "winchester" type drives is that they "landed" in a
> landing area where data wasn't recorded.  Ever notice in the BIOS setup
> where a "landing area" is specified, well, that's what it is for.  The
> "winchester" technology also included smaller heads (instead of the
> ceramic being about the size of a dime, they got to be a much smaller
> square shape (I don't know the exact details).  They also flew at a MUCH
> lower height.  The surface of the disk was also coated to allow the heads
> to land when the power was turned off.  In some manufacturers drives,
this
> caused problems because the heads would "stick" and the motor didn't have
> enough starting torque to get the spindle going.  This was the famous
> "sticktion" problem.

Yup!

> As for the air requirement, drives always needed air (still do) but with
> older drives the packs were removable, and to blow away the dust and
other
> crud filtered air was circulated in the assembly.  Now days drives are
put
> together "sealed" and to equalize the air pressure between the inner
drive
> and the atmosphere an "absolute" filter about the diameter of a cigarette
> is used.  If it didn't have it, it would probably "blow up" (quite
> dramatically, I suspect) if there was a large difference in air pressure
> from inside to outside (like when they are shipped in an unpressureized
> cargo hold of an aircraft).

Don't know about this theory but don't think that it's too probable. I
believe that most disk drive steel enclosures would tolerate a 10 to 15 PSI
(one atmosphere) pressure differential with little difficulty. 

The main reason for venting the enclosure is to ensure that the air inside
the drive is always the densest possible, to ensure that the heads will fly
at the greatest possible height. I'm sure that you'll acknowledge this once
you remember that specs for disk drives usually contain a spec for maximum
allowable altitude above sea level. Denver, Colorado just meets this spec.
at approx. 5200 feet above sea level.

Head crashes . . . . . . <GGGG>

My favourite was associated with a customer that had a dozen or so 2310
drives installed. These drives contained 10 platters and an appropriate
number of heads. This customer started to lose three or four disk packs a
week and most heads on the drive. The losses increased to 10 or more packs
(and their heads) a week and I was told to find and fix the problem.

Immediately next door to this customer was a shop whose business was
sharpening tungsten carbide tool bits for lathes and mills. The "cyclone"
separator was located behind their office, immediately adjacent to my
customer's air conditioning intake.

Wouldn't have been (actually wasn't for a year or two) too much of a
problem until a potato chip manufacturer located their business a block
away. The aroma was just dandy but the oil residue came downwind and
combined with the tungsten carbide dust to make the nicest disk polishing
compound I had ever seen.

Relocating the air intake to the front of the customer's building fixed the
problem.

and that's the way that I remember fixing these dogs <wide grin>!!


Bill Harnell

bharne@adss.on.ca



