In article <4s61nt$d0@falstaf.demon.co.uk>,
robin@falstaf.demon.co.uk (Robin Birch) writes:

:I am playing with some code that has to identify the system that
:it is running on and I am looking for the breakdown of the
:cpu identifier at 0000003e and the SID at 20040004.
:
:What I need is the meaning of each field and the various bit maps.
:Can somebody please look in the relevant manual.

  Most folks looking at the SID in this fashion are working on a
  license-enforcement scheme...

  The only portion of the SID that is part of the VAX Architecture
  is the uppermost byte, and this represents the processor family,
  or particular type of VAX microprocessor used in this system. 
  The rest of the SID is left up to the system implementation --
  sometimes it's the microcode revision, sometimes its a serial
  number (which can sometimes be set from the console and sometimes
  via switches on the backplane), and sometimes it contains the etch
  (revision) of the microprocessor.

  To differentiate members within the VAX family, one must look at
  both the SID and the XSID (on those VAX systems that _have_ the
  XSID register), and possibly at various sorts of other hardware
  present on the system.

  Far and away the easiest way to determine the model name of the
  system in use is to simply ask sys$getsyi for the processor model
  name, or to use the processor hardware model code.

