In <01bb801a$98d19e40$a99cd2cd@dharnell> "Dave Harnell"
<dharnell@in.on.ca> writes: 

>All of the above drives contained a "head load" mechanism which used a
>solenoid to force the heads against their platters once a specific
>rotational speed was reached, typically 70% of operational speed.
>Matter of fact, most 8 inch floppy drives used a similar mechanism. It
>was not until the introduction of 5 1/4 inch floppy drives that the
>head load mechanism was removed (probably to save money) and the heads
>allowed to permanently contact the disk surface. It was also around
>this time that media surfaces were "burnished", or polished, to
>prevent head wear.

Then it was discovered that having the heads on the surface all the
time STILL wasn't a great idea (not just wear, but it increased the
torque requried to spin up the disk), so the head load solenoids went
back in. (Mid-80's TEACs touted this feature.)-Wm



