>   83 BOOT SYS
>   -ESA0
>   ?54 RETRY
>   ?54 RETRY
>   ?54 RETRY and on and on...
> 
> I dont have a clue what is on the drive, how do I go about booting either 
> Ultrix or VMS? Is this a normal power up and diagnostics, I dont have a manual 
> either. What do I need to set boot and the bootflag to? Any help is greatly 
> appreciated.

        ESA0: is the ethernet port.   This  VAXstation is trying to do a
    MOP download over the network and boot into a VMScluster.  Nobody is
    answering  the dowload request, so it just sits there and tries  and
    tries again...

        In principle, you  could  boot  from  the  local  disk  with the
    command (for VMS),

    		>>>B DKA300

    However, the RZ23 is a  small  page-and-swap disk, too small to hold
    VMS  (at  a  version that supports the VS3100).   I'm  not  familiar
    enough with Ultrix to say whether that O/S would  fit,  but  100  MB
    ain't much!

        So you've got a few  choices.   One  is  to  add another disk of
    sufficient  size that you can load an operating system onto it.  The
    VS3100 does have an external SCSI port on the back, or you can maybe
    get something like a used RZ26 and mount it internally.  Then you've
    got to get the O/S loaded, which is most easily done  by  joining  a
    VMScluster,  at least temporarily.  Otherwise, you need some sort of
    tape drive...and tapes with the  O/S  on it.  Don't forget licensing
    here or loading an O/S won't do you a whole lot of good.  Ultrix may
    be  easier in terms of licensing issues (it may not be), but  you'll
    still need that additional disk and someone to  put  an  O/S  on  it
    before  you  mount  it  in your system.  Also, don't go putting some
    _large_  SCSI  disk  on  this  thing   as  the  system  disk.   Many
    discussions  here  (and elsewhere) have pointed out that the  VS3100
    can use at maximum 1.08 GB as a system disk (I think, someone please
    correct me 'cause I haven't really paid a lot of attention  to  this
    detail...).

        Or you could use it as a boat anchor...

            -Ken
