In article <ERIC.96Aug2160212@goonsquad.spies.com>, eric@goonsquad.spies.com (Eric Smith) writes:
> Is the TU81 a normal Massbus tape drive?

  No - it's TMSCP.

> If the TU81 won't do 18 bit, does its transport use a Pertec interface
> internally?  Could I replace the formatter with a TM03 to make it support
> 18 bit operation?

  There *might* be a place inside it where you could find a Pertec inter-
face. However, if you want to go that route, I'd start with the generic
CDC (MPI) version of the drive. The TU81 has a TMSCP formatter board in-
ternally and uses the LESI bus to talk to a KLESI adapter (either Unibus
or Q-bus).

  Even if you found an unformatted Pertec interface in there, you'd have
lots of problems using a TM03 - the TU81 changes tape speed depending on
how well the host is doing with keeping up, so you'd need to find the speed
signal and wire it to the TM to change the formatter's idea of tape IPS. I
don't know if the TM can handle those changes in real-time.

  If there's a PDP-10 version of the TU78/TU79, I'd suggest one of them
instead - faster drive (though more tempermental), closer interface to
what you want.

  OTOH, the vendors of DECsystem-compatible boxes (like Systems Concepts)
have managed to attach industry-standard disks and tapes to their boxes -
maybe some of their driver changes would help you use a standard drive?

