In article <4tr1l5$ess@agate.berkeley.edu>,
Peter Mardahl <peterm@landau.EECS.Berkeley.EDU> wrote:
>
>  Do Digital UNIX binaries run under Alpha/Linux?
>I vaguely remember that there was an effort to make this
>possible.
>
>  We're considering buying an Alpha, and this capability
>would be very helpful to us.

Generally the answer is "yes", with a few caveats:

 - Linux doesn't have any license manager.  Programs using that will
   probably fail, and I don't know if anybody has enough documentation
   to fix things like that. 

 - Linux doesn't support the Mach-specific system calls (negative system
   call numbers).  I've never seen any program use them, but they might
   exist anyway.  I suspect things like a threads package might use the
   Mach threads facilities.. 

 - Linux does not come with the Digital Unix shared libraries and the
   loader (/sbin/loader).  If you are using dynamically linked binaries
   (ie just about everything), you need to have a license for those and
   copy them over to the Linux box..  There are a few other Digital Unix
   specific things you need to have in your filesystem tree too (some
   extra symlinks and config files to make binaries that expect them
   happy). 

 - some Digital Unix specific low-level binaries do not work, and you
   have to use the Linux versions. This is mainly stuff like "ifconfig"
   etc, but also the X server, for example.  (X clients work fine, but
   the server does strange things directly on the hardware, so you have
   to use XFree86 instead which is probably the better server anyway). 

And finally:

 - A lot of the Digital UNIX binary compatibility has not been
   extensively tested.  I _used_ to run a all-OSF binary setup a long
   time ago, so I can only say that it has worked fine for me.  However,
   I've been using native binaries now for a long time, and I don't know
   how many people use Digital Unix binaries.  I know somebody was
   running the Digital Unix version of Netscape, because no Linux/alpha
   native version exists yet.  So even large and complex binaries DO
   work, but I can make no guarantees.  You may end up having to fix
   some specific problem yourself (you'll find people more than willing
   to help, though: we do want to be Digital UNIX binary compatible as
   far as humanly possible). 

Hope that helped (even though the end result of the above is "try it and
pray for the best" ;-),

		Linus



