In article <4tl339$6dg@kannews.ca.newbridge.com> Paul DeMone <pdemone@tundra.com> writes:

       Low power ECL is kind of an oxymoron.  The ECL gate (or in its
       simpler form, a CML gate) is basically a differential amp built
       with at least two BJTs with their emitters tied together to a
       common current source.  The amount of bias current will affect
       the intrinsic gate speed so it cannot be arbitrarily reduced.

The electronics hawks will correct me, I'm sure, but I believe that a
better way of explaining power consumption of ECL is to say that they
trade off power consumption for speed by not saturating the
transistors driving the outputs. Regular TTL/CMOS output stage
transistors are either totally on or off.  The power dissipation,
being given by U*I (voltage across the transistor times the current
through it), is low: in ON state, U=0, in OFF state I=0.  However,
changing their state requires an inrush of extra carriers.
Incidentally, this also means that TTL/CMOS chips require spikes of
power while switching.

ECL output transistors are always on, in a push-pull fashion; the
logic state is determined by which one in the pair is 'more on'. Thus,
the ECL gate consumes constant power, and requires less care in
decoupling and distributing  power.

