In article <DvoJHH.3A@draco.nova.edu>, kevin@ocean.nova.edu (Kevin  Kohler) writes:
:I currently have a Storageworks vertical cabinet (BA350-VA) attached to 
:a DEC 3000/400 workstation. I'd like to transfer the cabinet to an
:AlphaStation 400 4/233. 
:
:Has anyone hooked up such a StorageWorks cabinet to an AlphaStation 400 4/233?
:Word from DEC is that I'm left with .6m of cable length to work with (1.5m
:internal in the 4/233, .9m in the Sworks cabinet) for fast SCSI.
:A half meter barely gets me out the cabinet itself.  
:The AlphaStation has 2 internal disks; the Sworks has 4 disks and a 4mm DAT.
:
:Has anyone used a longer cable with success? 

  The longer one makes a SCSI bus, the flakier it typically gets.  (It
  is/was possible to create an entirely standards-compliant and entirely
  non-functional SCSI configuration -- though each revision of the SCSI
  specification does get successively better in this particular area.)

  You _might_ be successful with simply a longer cable, and you might end
  up with the `flakies'.  (Personally, I'm always looking for ways to
  _shorten_ the SCSI bus, not lengthen it.)  If you do choose this path,
  make _certain_ you use high-quality SCSI cables, and make certain you
  have solid connections.  (I do _not_ recommend this course.)

  If you want a (supported) longer SCSI bus connection, you'll want to go
  from a `single-ended' (SE) SCSI to a configuration using `differential'
  (D) SCSI, which means a SCSI controller capable of differential (KZPSA
  is a PCI controller capable of Fast-Wide-Differential (FWD) SCSI), or
  the use of one or more single-ended-SCSI-to-differential-SCSI converters
  such as the DWZZA.

  [I have _not_ checked to see if the BA350-SA/SB is supported `behind' a
  DWZZA-xx SCSI signal converter -- though it would appear one can convert
  from SE to D and then back to SE, and this _appears_ to be supported in
  the BA350-SA/SB, and this would give you 10+ meters of `D' SCSI cabling
  between the SE host SCSI and the SE BA350-SA/SB enclosure.  And support.]

  Information: http://www.storage.digital.com

  ------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Stephen 'Hoff' Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com
