don@rata.vuw.ac.nz (Don Stokes) writes:

>Of course an 8" drive spins all the time, whereas a 5.25" (and 3.5") drive
>spins only when being accessed.  [...]

>What happens if you keep the floppy spinning with the head loaded is
>this: you get a transparent floppy.  The heads will dig through the oxide
>down to the mylar within a day or two of operation.  (Think about this
>when committing vital data to floppies! 8-)

Another consequence of this is that the drive motor runs.  and runs.  and
runs...without ever stopping.  Not always a good idea...

Earlier this year we decommissioned the last of the IBM mainframes at
the shop.  The 3880 (disk control unit) boxes each had an 8" floppy
drive that contained the executable images that actually ran in the
controllers; these disks generally were used only during a power-up
cycle and in the event of an error condition for which detailed information
was to be recorded.  (Minor errors like overruns were merely counted, 
but hard errors resulted in detailed reports.)

On the average we might have a cold shutdown every 18 months or so (a
"cold shutdown" meaning that the system is closed and power turned
off).  The operations staff didn't like such events, because there
was a quite real probability that one or more of the 3880 boxes would
be unable to restart because the drive motor on the 8" floppy would
seize when it cooled down, because the bearings had been worn out of
spec but with the motor staying hot the clearances were sufficient 
to allow it to turn.

Oddly enough, we never had any problems with the twin 8" floppy drives
that were part of the 4381 CPU, nor with the drive in the 3820
communications controller.

Joe Morris / MITRE



