|> | |> because some people needed a non-GPLed implementation.  This library
|> | |> contains all the arbitrary-precision arithmetic needed to do public-key
|> | |> cryptography.
|> | 
|> | Interesting.  I was not aware of the mp/FGMP common interface.  
|> | Is there a general consensus about the broad suitability of 
|> | this?  [The fact that the library was re-implemented suggests yes...]
|> | If so, this may be the right interface to build the benchmark on.
|> 
|> I saw some interesting (or discouraging) results in the early days
|> of FGMP, indicating that while compatible the libs are not equal in
|> performance. For a benchmark where the source should be available, I
|> see no problem with mp. For a commercial application I would hope
|> that FGMP would be fast enough.
|> 
|> I thought libraries were under another form of license, though.


The significance, as I see it, is that if two equivalent libraries
exist, the interface is well-defined and reasonable to implement.
A particular vendor might choose to re-implement the library.
From an interoperability standpoint, the more equivalent libraries
there are, the better.

I hope, however, that implementors of any equivalent libraries 
are careful to use the same (7?) low-level arithmetic routines,
with the same interfaces, that the original library did, since
it is most likely that a vendor will want to optimize those
routines (only), and, that might make the basis for a good
benchmark.

