>>when committing vital data to floppies! 8-)
>
>Another consequence of this is that the drive motor runs.  and runs.  and
>runs...without ever stopping.  Not always a good idea...

During the late 70's/early 80's, you could buy little modules to
put in your 8" drives that would start them spinning when a head load
signal was received, and keep them spinning for 30 seconds afterward.
As I remember the internals, it was little more than a 555 or TTL
one-shot and a Triac.

This little gizmo didn't work with all 8" floppy controllers, but it
worked with most.  The delay in getting the motor spinning was much
longer than the usual delay for a head load, but this wasn't a huge
problem because in practice the controller doesn't mind waiting while
looking for a sector header.  

