davidsen@tmr.com (bill davidsen) writes:
>I saw some interesting (or discouraging) results in the early days
>of FGMP, indicating that while compatible the libs are not equal in
>performance. For a benchmark where the source should be available, I
>see no problem with mp. For a commercial application I would hope
>that FGMP would be fast enough.

FGMP is substantially inferior to GMP in performance, but its APIs are
compatible.  It was created as an answer to people who objected to
non-GPLed programs (RSAREF, in this case) with GMP interfaces, claiming
that this was an invalid attempt to get around the GPL.  (The GPL says
that you must distribute the full source of a program that uses GPL'ed
code and not add any restrictions incompatible with the GPL, such as
forbidding export).  The FSF claims you can't get around this by shipping
your program in two pieces and having the user do the link, and their
legal argument was enough to convince NeXT's lawyers not to try this trick
with their gcc-based Objective-C compiler.

In the GMP battle, lots of folks jumped on FSF, accusing them of claiming
an interface copyright.  FSF answered no, they were not claiming the
interface, and in ensuing discussion said that their objection would have
no force if another library with the same interface existed.  Hence FGMP
was created, not with the intention that it actually be used, but so that
people could rip out FGMP and put in GMP.

Folks who wish to argue about GPL-related issues should go to
gnu.misc.discuss, but the point is that no attempt was made to
optimize FGMP's performance, only to make it correct, and it is
known to be a lot worse than GMP; also that FSF no longer has any
objection to non-free programs using GMP-compatible interfaces now
that FGMP exists.


