>> 
>> >*Moving* head on a drum? <rotfl>
>> 
>> Yep.  The Fastrand had heads on a moveable boom.  No joke.
>
>Of course.  The heads have less mass than the drum.  Therefore it
>takes less energy to move the heads, the device gets up to speed
>faster, and the whole thing does less damage when freaked.
>What's the problem here ? :-)

Remember, the FastRand was intended to behave much like a disk drive.  As
of the time I leaped from Sperry 1100s to the Eunuchs environment (about
10 years ago, now), all disk storage was still "FastRand" Formatted w/
28 word (112 9bit bytes) sectors and a 1792 word "track".  There are 64
of these "tracks" to a "position" (a position of the fastrand's "comb"
of heads).

Note:	some of the number above may not have been remembered correctly.

I've heard apocryphal stories about FastRands, like the story that one
was mounted on a Navy ship and would not allow the ship to make turns
until it was spun down.  Other stories were just as entertaining.

Don't laugh, though-  it *worked*.  Sure, they retained the fastrand
format on disk packs, but that wasn't particulary destructive because of
the 36bit architecture of the 1100s-  And I'll always have a warm place
in my heart for the 1100 series (including the Array Processor).

